"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,529\nThe Learning Brain. Series 5: Belonging\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,331\nEpisode 10\n\n3\n00:00:07,411 --> 00:00:09,687\nStructure is inclusive\n\n4\n00:00:12,487 --> 00:00:15,484\nWho gains most when a lecture course becomes active?\n\n5\n00:00:15,951 --> 00:00:23,829\nA large analysis of college science and math courses suggests an answer: the students most often left behind.\n\n6\n00:00:23,999 --> 00:00:30,255\nIn 2020, Elli Theobald and colleagues pooled studies of undergraduate STEM courses.\n\n7\n00:00:30,632 --> 00:00:33,461\nThey compared traditional lecturing with active learning,\n\n8\n00:00:33,718 --> 00:00:36,498\nfor underrepresented and low income students,\n\n9\n00:00:36,747 --> 00:00:38,064\nand for everyone else.\n\n10\n00:00:38,548 --> 00:00:43,058\nExam data came from fifteen studies and over nine thousand students.\n\n11\n00:00:43,434 --> 00:00:48,277\nPass rates came from twenty six studies and over forty four thousand students.\n\n12\n00:00:48,447 --> 00:00:50,372\nActive learning helped everyone,\n\n13\n00:00:50,593 --> 00:00:53,096\nand it helped underrepresented students more.\n\n14\n00:00:53,644 --> 00:00:58,491\nOn average, it narrowed the gap in exam scores by thirty three percent,\n\n15\n00:00:58,832 --> 00:01:02,184\nand the gap in passing rates by forty five percent.\n\n16\n00:01:02,354 --> 00:01:03,580\nIntensity mattered.\n\n17\n00:01:04,085 --> 00:01:09,048\nIn classes where students were actively engaged for at least two thirds of class time,\n\n18\n00:01:09,345 --> 00:01:12,073\nthe exam gap shrank by forty two percent,\n\n19\n00:01:12,403 --> 00:01:15,056\nand the passing gap by seventy six percent.\n\n20\n00:01:15,226 --> 00:01:18,464\nThe authors call their explanation heads and hearts.\n\n21\n00:01:18,800 --> 00:01:23,373\nHeads: deliberate practice, with frequent problem solving and feedback.\n\n22\n00:01:23,780 --> 00:01:28,693\nHearts: a classroom that tells every student they belong and can succeed.\n\n23\n00:01:29,125 --> 00:01:31,029\nGood structure delivers both.\n\n24\n00:01:31,199 --> 00:01:32,172\nSome caution.\n\n25\n00:01:32,615 --> 00:01:37,835\nThese studies compared existing courses rather than randomly assigned classrooms,\n\n26\n00:01:38,071 --> 00:01:39,504\nand there were not many of them.\n\n27\n00:01:39,917 --> 00:01:43,600\nAnd how much class time you give to active work seems to matter.\n\n28\n00:01:43,770 --> 00:01:44,514\nFor teaching.\n\n29\n00:01:44,943 --> 00:01:48,822\nGive students frequent, low stakes problems to work on in class.\n\n30\n00:01:49,122 --> 00:01:51,618\nUse structured group work with clear roles.\n\n31\n00:01:51,936 --> 00:01:53,441\nCall on students in fair,\n\n32\n00:01:53,461 --> 00:01:56,134\npredictable ways, with time to think first.\n\n33\n00:01:56,457 --> 00:01:59,429\nAnd aim for more class time active,\n\n34\n00:01:59,845 --> 00:02:01,713\nnot less.\n\n35\n00:02:01,950 --> 00:02:04,964\nOne tip to share with students: join a study group and work problems out loud.\n\n36\n00:02:05,134 --> 00:02:07,312\nStructure is not the opposite of care.\n\n37\n00:02:07,765 --> 00:02:11,413\nDone well, structure is how care reaches every student.\n\n"
